Exercise reduces blood cortisol levels — the primary stress hormone — for up to 24 hours after a single session, according to studies published in Psychoneuroendocrinology.
Aerobic exercise triggers release of endorphins and endocannabinoids, producing the well-documented 'runner's high' that blunts emotional distress.
A 2019 meta-analysis in JAMA Psychiatry reviewed 1.2 million Americans and found that exercising people reported 1.5 fewer 'bad mental health days' per month than sedentary people.
Regular exercise downregulates the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis, meaning the body's stress response becomes less reactive over time.
